Use three pointers: walk the list once, and flip each node's `next` to point at the node before it.

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

typedef struct Node {
    int data;
    struct Node *next;
} Node;

Node *reverse(Node *head) {
    Node *prev = NULL;
    Node *curr = head;

    while (curr != NULL) {
        Node *next = curr->next;  // save the rest of the list
        curr->next = prev;        // flip the link
        prev = curr;              // advance prev
        curr = next;              // advance curr
    }
    return prev;                  // prev is the new head
}
```

**Usage**

```c
Node *push(Node *head, int value) {
    Node *n = malloc(sizeof *n);
    n->data = value;
    n->next = head;
    return n;
}

int main(void) {
    Node *head = NULL;
    for (int i = 5; i >= 1; i--) head = push(head, i);  // 1 2 3 4 5

    head = reverse(head);                               // 5 4 3 2 1

    for (Node *p = head; p; p = p->next) printf("%d ", p->data);
    printf("\n");

    while (head) { Node *t = head->next; free(head); head = t; }
    return 0;
}
```

**How it works**

For `1 -> 2 -> 3`:

| step | prev | curr | result |
|------|------|------|--------|
| start | NULL | 1 | |
| 1 | 1 | 2 | `NULL <- 1` |
| 2 | 2 | 3 | `NULL <- 1 <- 2` |
| 3 | 3 | NULL | `NULL <- 1 <- 2 <- 3` |

When `curr` hits `NULL`, `prev` points to the old tail, which is now the head.

- **Complexity:** O(n) time, O(1) extra space.
- **Edge cases:** An empty list (`head == NULL`) and a single node both work with no special handling.
- **Reassign the result:** `head = reverse(head);` is required, because the old head becomes the tail.

**Recursive version** (O(n) stack depth, so it can overflow on very long lists):

```c
Node *reverse_rec(Node *head) {
    if (head == NULL || head->next == NULL) return head;
    Node *new_head = reverse_rec(head->next);
    head->next->next = head;
    head->next = NULL;
    return new_head;
}
```

I'd use the iterative version in practice.

